---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
---

<Doc
  title="The OTAKU Manifesto"
  kicker="Chapter 01 — the constitution"
  description="Five pillars of Fluid Structure Design: discipline, composition, a protected core, silent infrastructure and universal contracts."
  path="/manifesto"
>
  <p class="lede">
    OTAKU is an acronym, and every letter is a constraint. A constraint is not a
    preference: it is a test that a piece of code must pass before it is allowed to
    exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    The five pillars are ordered on purpose. Discipline is first because nothing else
    survives without it. Contracts are last because they are the only thing the
    outside world is allowed to see.
  </p>

  <div class="pillars">
    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar__letter">O</div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="pillar__name">Own your Discipline</h3>
        <p class="pillar__short">Be strict with yourself.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar__letter">T</div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="pillar__name">Tools for Composition</h3>
        <p class="pillar__short">Compose like Unix.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar__letter">A</div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="pillar__name">Armor the Core</h3>
        <p class="pillar__short">Protect the heart of the business.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar__letter">K</div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="pillar__name">Keep Infrastructure Silent</h3>
        <p class="pillar__short">Infrastructure is just a detail.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar__letter">U</div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="pillar__name">Universal Language &amp; Contracts</h3>
        <p class="pillar__short">Speak the user's language.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>O — Own your Discipline</h2>

  <p>
    Quality is not an accident; it is the result of intentional restriction.
    Restriction is the only path to liberation, because every option you keep open is
    a decision you postponed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The nine rules of Object Calisthenics are mandatory. They are mechanical on
    purpose: a rule that depends on taste cannot be reviewed, automated or argued
    with.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Only one level of indentation per method.</li>
    <li>Do not use the <code>else</code> keyword.</li>
    <li>Wrap all primitives and strings.</li>
    <li>First-class collections.</li>
    <li>One dot per line.</li>
    <li>Do not abbreviate.</li>
    <li>Keep entities small: 50 lines per class, 10 files per package.</li>
    <li>No class with more than two instance variables.</li>
    <li>No getters, setters or public properties.</li>
  </ol>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>Write code that never needs “fixing” because it was built under absolute constraint.</p>
  </Note>

  <p>
    <a href="/guide/object-calisthenics">Apply the nine rules →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>T — Tools for Composition</h2>

  <p>
    Like Unix tools, each component does one thing and does it well. Architecture is
    not a diagram of layers; it is a set of small, replaceable parts connected by
    clear inputs and outputs.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the rule of modularity applied to application flow: input enters through
    an adapter, traverses the core, and exits. Side effects live at the edges. The
    middle is a data pipe with no knowledge of where the data came from.
  </p>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>
      Treat your application flow as a data pipe: input enters through adapters,
      traverses the core, and exits — keeping logic isolated from side effects.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <p>
    <a href="/guide/composition">Compose with parts and pipes →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>A — Armor the Core</h2>

  <p>
    The business logic is the most valuable asset in the system and the only part
    that cannot be bought, generated or replaced. It must be protected from external
    change.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dependencies point inwards only. The heart — entities and use cases — never knows
    about frameworks, databases, HTTP or external APIs. When a framework upgrade
    breaks compilation, the blast radius should be adapters, never the domain.
  </p>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>
      The heart — use cases and entities — must never know about frameworks,
      databases or external APIs.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <p>
    <a href="/guide/protect-the-core">Armor the core →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>K — Keep Infrastructure Silent</h2>

  <p>
    Databases, mailers and message brokers are secondary details. Persistence is
    treated as an in-memory collection through the Repository pattern, which hides
    SQL, transactions and vendor quirks behind a domain-shaped interface.
  </p>

  <p>
    Entities are plain objects — POPO, POCO, POJO, it does not matter what the
    language calls them. They are completely ignorant of how they are saved or
    transmitted.
  </p>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>
      Your entities are plain objects, completely ignorant of how they are saved or
      transmitted.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <p>
    <a href="/guide/silent-infrastructure">Keep infrastructure silent →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>U — Universal Language &amp; Contracts</h2>

  <p>
    Ubiquitous Language bridges business and code: if the expert says “settlement”,
    the class is not called <code>PaymentResult</code>. Design-first contracts make
    that language explicit and testable, and become the immutable technical promise
    that lets systems communicate.
  </p>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>
      If the code does not read like the business expert speaks, or if the contract
      is bypassed, the system is broken.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <p>
    <a href="/guide/contracts">Write contracts, not surprises →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>How the pillars enforce each other</h2>

  <p>
    They are not independent virtues. Discipline is what makes composition possible:
    a 300-line class cannot be replaced. Composition is what makes the core
    defensible: a monolith cannot hide its dependencies. A protected core is what
    makes infrastructure genuinely optional. And contracts are the only reason any
    of it can be verified from outside.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remove one pillar and the others become ceremony. Keep all five and the system
    becomes boring — in the way bridges are boring.
  </p>

  <Note label="Next" variant="warn">
    <p>
      A philosophy that cannot be classified is not applied. The next chapter defines
      the three shapes a repository is allowed to take.
    </p>
  </Note>
</Doc>
